---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Doc from '../components/legal/Doc.astro';
const updated = '29 October 2026';
// Drafts stay out of search until counsel signs off and every placeholder is filled (design/legal/README.md).
const draft = true;
---

<Base title="Acceptable use policy · Comarket" description="What brands, creators and visitors may not do on Comarket, how sales must be earned, and how to report content." stage="none" noindex={draft}>
  <Doc
    title="Acceptable use policy"
    lede="Sales on Comarket have to be earned honestly. These rules apply to everyone: brands, creators and visitors."
    updated={updated}
    draft={draft}
    current="/acceptable-use"
  >
    <h2>1. Who this applies to</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">1.1</span>This policy is part of the <a href="/terms">Terms of use</a>, the
      <a href="/brand-terms">Brand Agreement</a> and the <a href="/creator-terms">Creator Agreement</a>. It applies to anyone
      who uses Comarket, its storefronts, hubs and links, and to anyone acting for them. "Comarket", "we" and "us" mean
      [[LEGAL ENTITY NAME]].
    </p>

    <h2>2. Earning sales honestly</h2>
    <p><span class="n">2.1</span>A sale must come from a real buyer who chose to buy after seeing honest content. You must not:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>buy through your own link,</b> or have someone in your household, an employee, or anyone acting for you do so;</li>
      <li><b>pay, reward or pressure buyers</b> to buy, for example by sharing your commission, offering cashback, or running a giveaway or contest that needs a purchase, unless the Offer allows it and the law permits it;</li>
      <li><b>stuff cookies or force clicks:</b> load links in hidden frames or pixels, auto-redirect, use pop-ups or pop-unders, or use browser extensions, toolbars or adware to claim sales;</li>
      <li><b>hijack sales,</b> for example by catching people already on the way to the brand, or by typosquatting;</li>
      <li><b>leak codes or links</b> to coupon, cashback, deal or voucher sites, unless the Offer allows it;</li>
      <li><b>bid on a brand's name</b> or trademarks, or close misspellings of them, in paid search or social ads, or use them in domain or account names, without the brand's written permission;</li>
      <li><b>use fake identities,</b> several accounts, stolen cards, or other tricks to get around the new-customer check or to earn on your own purchases;</li>
      <li><b>inflate your audience</b> with bots, fake followers, bought engagement or engagement pods, or misstate your audience to brands; or</li>
      <li><b>tamper with tracking:</b> strip, swap or fake the storefront reference on a checkout.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Honest content</h2>
    <p><span class="n">3.1</span>Anyone promoting an Offer must:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>disclose the connection</b> clearly in every piece of content, as section 7 of the Creator Agreement describes;</li>
      <li><b>tell the truth:</b> only real opinions and real experience, and only claims about a product, its price or its results that the Offer allows and the brand can back up;</li>
      <li><b>not post fake reviews</b> or testimonials, or reviews you paid for or were given something for without saying so;</li>
      <li><b>not pose</b> as the brand, its staff or an ordinary customer;</li>
      <li><b>not use scarcity or price claims</b> that aren't true, such as a "last day" that isn't, or a "was" price that never applied; and</li>
      <li><b>not target children,</b> or promote in content made for children.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.2</span>Claims about health, medical results, weight loss, money, investments or earnings need particular
      care, and may be made only where the Offer expressly allows them and the law permits them.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Messages and spam</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">4.1</span>Don't send links by unsolicited email, text, direct message or comment, or post them where a
      community's rules forbid it. Follow the laws on electronic marketing where you and your audience are, such as CAN-SPAM and
      the TCPA in the US, PECR in the UK, the EU ePrivacy rules and CASL in Canada. Email only people who agreed to hear from
      you, and let them stop.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Content and products we don't allow</h2>
    <p><span class="n">5.1</span>No one may use Comarket for content that:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>is illegal, or promotes illegal activity;</li>
      <li>infringes someone's copyright, trademark, privacy, publicity or other rights;</li>
      <li>sexually exploits or endangers children, in any form;</li>
      <li>is sexually explicit;</li>
      <li>harasses, threatens or incites violence against anyone, or attacks people for a protected characteristic;</li>
      <li>is deceptive, including impersonation and manipulated media presented as real; or</li>
      <li>contains malware, phishing or anything designed to harm devices or steal data.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.2</span>Brands may not offer, and creators may not promote through Comarket: weapons and ammunition; drugs,
      including cannabis, and drug paraphernalia; tobacco, vaping and nicotine products; alcohol, except where we agree in
      writing and the law allows; gambling, lotteries and sweepstakes that need payment; prescription medicines; adult
      products and services; investments, trading, crypto-assets, loans and other financial products that need a licence,
      unless the brand holds it and we agree in writing; multi-level marketing and "get rich" schemes; counterfeit goods; or
      any product or business that Stripe's restricted business list excludes.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Using the service</h2>
    <p><span class="n">6.1</span>Don't:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>scrape, crawl or collect data from Comarket, its storefronts or hubs by automated means, except search engines that follow our robots rules;</li>
      <li>try to get into accounts or systems you aren't allowed into, or test our security without our written permission (report weaknesses to [[CONTACT EMAIL]]; we welcome good-faith reports);</li>
      <li>overload, disrupt or get around rate limits, bot checks or other protections;</li>
      <li>copy, reverse engineer or resell Comarket, its storefront designs or its data, except as the law allows; or</li>
      <li>use what you learn about brands, creators or buyers on Comarket for anything other than working together on Comarket.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>7. Reporting content</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.1</span>Anyone can report content on Comarket that they believe is illegal or breaks this policy, by
      writing to [[CONTACT EMAIL]] with: the address (URL) of the content; why you think it is illegal or breaks this policy;
      your name and email address (you may leave these out if you are reporting child sexual abuse material); and a statement
      that you believe in good faith that the report is accurate and complete.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.2</span>We confirm receipt, review reports promptly and with care, and tell you what we decided. If a
      decision uses automated tools, we say so.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.3</span>That address is also our single point of contact for authorities and for users under the EU Digital
      Services Act. We correspond in English and [[CONTACT LANGUAGES]].
    </p>

    <h2>8. What we do about breaches</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">8.1</span>We may investigate any suspected breach, and we may use automated tools to help find fraud. Our
      response is proportionate to how serious the breach is, how often it has happened and whether it was intentional. We may:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>warn you, or ask you to fix a post or a storefront;</li>
      <li>remove or hide content, or take a storefront down;</li>
      <li>refuse to count sales, and withhold or reverse earnings or fees on the sales affected;</li>
      <li>suspend or end an account under the relevant agreement; and</li>
      <li>report illegal activity to the authorities.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <span class="n">8.2</span>When we restrict content or an account, we tell the person affected what we did, why, which rule
      or law it rests on, whether it was detected or decided with automated tools, and how to challenge it. You can challenge a
      decision for 6 months through the complaints process in <a href="/terms#part-b-general-terms">Part B of the Terms of
      use</a>, and a person will review it. We may stop handling reports from people who often send reports that are clearly
      unfounded, after warning them.
    </p>
  </Doc>
  <Footer />
</Base>
